                'infographic_url' => 'coconut-nursery-lifecycle',
                'audio_tracks' => [
                    'en' => '../academy_uploads/audio/farm-operations-mgr-en.mp3',
                    'pcm' => '../academy_uploads/audio/farm-operations-mgr-pcm.mp3',
                    'ha' => '../academy_uploads/audio/farm-operations-mgr-ha.mp3',
                    'yo' => '../academy_uploads/audio/farm-operations-mgr-yo.mp3',
                    'ig' => '../academy_uploads/audio/farm-operations-mgr-ig.mp3',
                ],
                'content' => "[TAKEAWAY]Production planning is the art of keeping the farm's age structure in balance. A healthy stand maintains young palms, mature palms, and rolling replacement of senile trees.[/TAKEAWAY]\n\nProduction planning is the art of keeping the farm's age structure in balance. A healthy coconut stand has palms at every stage: young palms coming into bearing, mature palms carrying the harvest, and a small share of senile palms earmarked for replacement. An unbalanced stand produces boom-and-bust harvests.\n\n[WARNING]Allowing senile palms to exceed 25% of plantation stands causes catastrophic multi-year revenue slumps that take 5 to 7 years to reverse.[/WARNING]\n\nBegin with a stand census. Count palms by age class and block, and mark missing, diseased, or dead palms. The census reveals your true productive capacity and your replacement needs. A farm where more than a quarter of palms are senile needs a replanting program; a farm with too many young palms needs patience and interim income from intercropping.\n\n[PROTIP]Plan rolling replanting of senile palms annually using certified seedlings so total plantation yield never experiences sudden drop-offs.[/PROTIP]\n\nPlan replanting as a rolling program rather than a single event. Replace a small number of senile palms each year, funded by current harvests, so production never collapses. When replanting, use certified seedlings from a reputable nursery, and plant at the start of the rains with a full watering plan for the first dry season.\n\nIntercropping is the classic tool for cash flow during the immature years. Short-cycle crops such as vegetables or legumes earn income while palms mature, but place them outside the palm basin so they do not compete for water or harbour pests. Manage intercrop residues as mulch or compost to feed the soil rather than waste it.\n\nTrack yield per block per harvest, and compare against your own history and against realistic benchmarks for your variety and rainfall. Falling yields are a signal to investigate: nutrition, water, pests, or aging palms. Production planning is never finished; it is the monthly habit of reading the stand and adjusting before problems compound.",
