                'infographic_url' => 'coconut-nursery-lifecycle',
                'audio_tracks' => [
                    'en' => '../academy_uploads/audio/farm-operations-mgr-en.mp3',
                    'pcm' => '../academy_uploads/audio/farm-operations-mgr-pcm.mp3',
                    'ha' => '../academy_uploads/audio/farm-operations-mgr-ha.mp3',
                    'yo' => '../academy_uploads/audio/farm-operations-mgr-yo.mp3',
                    'ig' => '../academy_uploads/audio/farm-operations-mgr-ig.mp3',
                ],
                'content' => "[TAKEAWAY]Inputs are the largest recurring cost on coconut farms. Source exclusively from verified suppliers, calibrate application equipment, and record every batch applied.[/TAKEAWAY]\n\nInputs are the largest recurring cost on most coconut farms, and the easiest place to lose money. Procurement discipline means buying the right product, at the right time, from a verified source, and applying it at the right rate. Each of those rights is a decision point where quality and cost are won or lost.\n\n[CHECKLIST]Procure registered inputs strictly from accredited suppliers on the NATCODEV marketplace\nRetain physical supplier receipts and product batch labels in farm audit binder\nCalibrate sprayers and measurement buckets prior to field distribution\nLog exact application date, chemical/fertilizer dose, block ID, and applicator name[/CHECKLIST]\n\n[WARNING]Never over-apply chemical fertilizers without soil testing. Excessive dosing wastes capital, risks nitrogen burn, and harms delicate soil biology.[/WARNING]\n\nStart with a procurement calendar tied to your production plan. Fertiliser is typically applied before the rains so nutrients are available during growth flushes; pest control follows scouting rather than the calendar. Buying inputs in a panic, at the last hour, costs more and invites substitution with whatever is available rather than whatever is best.\n\nSource inputs from verified suppliers. On the NATCODEV marketplace, prefer accredited providers with clear product listings, formulations, and stock confirmation. Check that the product is labelled and registered for its stated purpose, and keep the receipt and label with your farm records. Counterfeit or mislabelled inputs are expensive failures disguised as savings: they cost the price of the product plus the lost season.\n\nApplication is where plans meet reality. Calibrate equipment, measure by the labelled rate, and apply to the labelled area. Record every application: date, product, quantity, block, and applicator. Records protect you during audits and residue checks, and they let you evaluate whether an input actually worked by comparing treated blocks against your yield logs.\n\nFinally, resist the temptation to over-apply. More fertiliser is not more yield; excess is wasted money, can harm soil life, and can run off into waterways. The professional manager buys enough, applies it correctly, and proves it with records.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Labour Supervision and Task Governance',
                'summary' => 'Assign, evidence, and verify field work that pays correctly.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'video_url' => '../academy_uploads/videos/certified-coconut-farm-operations-manager-one-minute-brief.html',
