                'audio_tracks' => [
                    'en' => '../academy_uploads/audio/farm-operations-mgr-en.mp3',
                    'pcm' => '../academy_uploads/audio/farm-operations-mgr-pcm.mp3',
                    'ha' => '../academy_uploads/audio/farm-operations-mgr-ha.mp3',
                    'yo' => '../academy_uploads/audio/farm-operations-mgr-yo.mp3',
                    'ig' => '../academy_uploads/audio/farm-operations-mgr-ig.mp3',
                ],
                'content' => "[TAKEAWAY]Farm labour is managed through structured tasks, clear safety equipment standards, and photographic completion evidence before payroll settlement.[/TAKEAWAY]\n\nFarm labour is managed through tasks, not through hope. Task governance means every field job has an owner, a category, a location, an expected duration, and the evidence required to confirm it was done. That structure is what keeps workers safe, farms productive, and payroll honest.\n\n[STEPS]1. Task Specification: Issue written task card detailing block number, activity, and required PPE.\n2. Morning Safety Briefing: Inspect tools, ladders, cutting poles, and buddy assignments.\n3. Spot-Check Supervision: Walk active blocks mid-shift to correct techniques and ensure hydration.\n4. Verification Signoff: Inspect photographic proof and work completion logs prior to daily payroll approval.[/STEPS]\n\n[PROTIP]Brief workers on safety and listen to their morning feedback. They walk the stands daily and spot crown rot or rhinoceros beetle boreholes before anyone else.[/PROTIP]\n\nAssign tasks in writing, even if the writing is a phone message confirmed in the task list. State the category of work, because the category determines the safety requirements and the evidence standard. A worker who knows the category knows what protection to wear and what photo or count to submit at the end.\n\nSupervise by exception and by routine. Routine supervision means spot-checks during the day: walk the plots, compare the work to the assignment, and correct method problems early. Exception supervision means investigating anything that deviates: a task that ran long, a plot that looks wrong, a worker who reports a problem. Early correction is cheap; late correction is a harvest's worth of loss.\n\nEvidence and payment are linked. Task records confirm attendance and work done, so payroll can be settled without arguments. Verify the evidence before paying: a photo of the planted plot with a landmark, a count of palms treated, a harvest weight. If evidence is missing, pay the worker fairly but fix the process, because missing evidence is a supervision failure, not a worker failure.\n\nTreat workers as your most important partners. Brief them on safety, pay promptly, and listen to their reports; they see the farm daily and notice pest outbreaks, water stress, and fence damage before anyone else. A well-supervised team is the cheapest input on the farm.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Harvest, Post-Harvest and Quality Control',
                'summary' => 'Protect the quality your farming earned, from bunch to buyer.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'video_url' => '../academy_uploads/videos/certified-coconut-farm-operations-manager-one-minute-brief.html',
                'infographic_url' => 'coconut-nursery-lifecycle',
                'audio_tracks' => [
                    'en' => '../academy_uploads/audio/farm-operations-mgr-en.mp3',
                    'pcm' => '../academy_uploads/audio/farm-operations-mgr-pcm.mp3',
                    'ha' => '../academy_uploads/audio/farm-operations-mgr-ha.mp3',
                    'yo' => '../academy_uploads/audio/farm-operations-mgr-yo.mp3',
                    'ig' => '../academy_uploads/audio/farm-operations-mgr-ig.mp3',
                ],
                'content' => "[TAKEAWAY]Quality is decided at harvest and preserved post-harvest. Proper maturity sorting, gentle handling, and elevated drying prevent discounting and mould.[/TAKEAWAY]\n\nQuality is decided at harvest and preserved afterwards. A well-farmed estate can still earn a poor price if harvesting and handling are careless, because cracked, bruised, or mouldy nuts are discounted or rejected. The manager's job is to make quality control a routine, not a reaction.\n\n[CHECKLIST]Harvest at target maturity (green for tender water; brown husk with solid water slosh for copra/processing)\nLower bunches gently via pole or rope; never drop mature nuts onto rocks or hard surfaces\nImmediately quarantine and segregate drop-nuts from first-grade hand-harvested nuts\nStore harvested nuts off ground on ventilated slatted pallets under shaded canopy[/CHECKLIST]\n\n[PROTIP]Close the loop with a detailed quality log: grade, count, weight, and dispatch photos. This log protects against buyer delivery disputes.[/PROTIP]\n\nHarvest at the right maturity for the intended market. Drinking nuts are picked younger; processing and planting nuts need full maturity with a thick brown husk. Train pickers to judge maturity by husk colour and nut sound, and to harvest by pole or climbing technique rather than shaking palms or dropping bunches. Rough handling cracks shells and invites rot.\n\nAfter harvest, sort before transport. Remove cracked, damaged, diseased, and immature nuts, and keep drop-nuts separate from harvested nuts; they are lower grade and can carry contamination. De-husk promptly when the market requires it, and handle nuts gently throughout, because every bruise is a discount at the weighing scale.\n\nDrying and storage protect value. Keep nuts dry and shaded; moisture and heat drive mould, and mould can produce toxins that make nuts unsafe and unsellable. Store off the ground on racks or pallets in a ventilated area, and follow buyer-specific handling, fumigation, or certification requirements for export or premium contracts.\n\nClose the loop with a quality log: grade, count, weight, and condition at dispatch, plus photos. The log is your evidence in disputes and your baseline for improvement. Over time, quality control data shows which blocks and which handlers deliver the best nuts, and that is where you focus next season's investment.",
            ],
            [
