                    'ig' => '../academy_uploads/audio/field-agent-verification-ig.mp3',
                ],
                'content' => "[INFOGRAPHIC:field-agent-gps-audit|Farm Verification Protocol & Field Standards]\n\n[TAKEAWAY]Field agents are the eyes of the cooperative. Every farm verification requires stable GPS coordinates, daylight photos, and grower identity confirmation.[/TAKEAWAY]\n\nA field agent is the platform's eyes on the ground. Every claim a grower makes, a provider declares, or a farm records eventually meets the verification standard that the field agent enforces. Understanding what verification means, and what you are accountable for, is the foundation of this certification.\n\n[STEPS]1. Identity Confirmation: Verify grower national ID against platform registry details.\n2. Location Polygon: Record multi-point open-sky GPS boundary coordinates.\n3. Visual Evidence: Take clear daylight photos of entrance landmarks, bearing palms, and seedlings.\n4. Defensible Submission: Submit evidence pack same-day with objective descriptive notes.[/STEPS]\n\n[PROTIP]Wait for open-sky GPS fix accuracy to drop below 5 meters before recording coordinates, and always photograph a permanent entrance landmark.[/PROTIP]\n\nVerification is not a formality; it is an evidence-based confirmation that a person, farm, or business is real, reachable, and accurately described. Your job is to confirm three things: identity (the person or business is who they claim), location (the farm or depot exists where it is registered), and accuracy (the declared details match reality). Each of the three requires its own evidence.\n\nYou act on behalf of the cooperative and its buyers, so your independence matters. You verify facts, you do not sell outcomes. A verification that passes a farm that should fail damages every honest grower in the program, because it dilutes the value of the verification badge that buyers rely on. Treat every visit with the same rigor, whether the farm is a neighbour's or a stranger's.\n\nKnow the limits of your authority. You collect evidence and report facts; approvals, rejections, and account changes are decided by the verification team after review. If a situation is ambiguous, document what you saw and escalate rather than guessing. A well-documented escalation is a success; an undocumented guess is a liability.\n\nFinally, plan each visit before leaving: review the profile, the pending verification items, and the visit objective. Arrive knowing what must be confirmed, what evidence to capture, and what to do if the visit fails. Preparation is what separates a professional agent from a person with a phone.",
