                    'ig' => '../academy_uploads/audio/field-agent-verification-ig.mp3',
                ],
                'content' => "[INFOGRAPHIC:field-agent-gps-audit|Farm GPS Polygon & Photographic Evidence Protocol]\n\n[TAKEAWAY]GPS polygon evidence proves the farm boundary exists and verifies that the field agent physically walked the land. Weak or fabricated GPS points trigger automatic system audit flags.[/TAKEAWAY]\n\nCapture GPS coordinates at the farm's identifiable point: the main entrance, the farmhouse, or a named landmark at the plot. Stand still long enough for the phone to get a stable fix, usually fifteen to thirty seconds in open sky; a moving or indoor fix can be off by hundreds of metres. The coordinate should place you at the farm, not on the nearest road.\n\n[STEPS]1. Open Sky Fix: Wait 15–30 seconds until GPS device accuracy reading drops below 5 metres.\n2. 4-Corner Boundary: Stand at all 4 property corners to capture the complete boundary polygon.\n3. Landmark Linkage: Photograph permanent farm entrance or structure with GPS coordinates embedded.\n4. Avoid Moving Captures: Never capture coordinates from a moving motorcycle or vehicle.[/STEPS]\n\n[WARNING]Fabricating, guessing, or recycling GPS coordinates is an instant disqualification from the NATCODEV Field Agent Network and triggers an automated fraud escalation.[/WARNING]\n\nUnderstand accuracy expectations. Consumer GPS is typically accurate to a few metres outdoors in open sky, but much worse under dense canopy, near buildings, or in bad weather. If the fix is poor, wait, move to open ground, or note the reduced accuracy in your report. Never fabricate or guess a coordinate; a false coordinate is a serious integrity breach.\n\nFollow the platform's capture flow exactly: it timestamps the reading and links it to the visit record. If the app shows a low-accuracy warning, resolve it before leaving. When your GPS evidence is precise and tied to a landmark photo, the verification team can approve the visit without a single follow-up question.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Photo Evidence and Documentation',
                'summary' => 'Photographs that tell the true story of the farm or depot.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'video_url' => '../academy_uploads/videos/field-agent-verification-and-gps-evidence-certification-one-minute-brief.html',
                'infographic_url' => 'field-agent-gps-audit',
                'audio_tracks' => [
                    'en' => '../academy_uploads/audio/field-agent-verification-en.mp3',
                    'pcm' => '../academy_uploads/audio/field-agent-verification-pcm.mp3',
                    'ha' => '../academy_uploads/audio/field-agent-verification-ha.mp3',
                    'yo' => '../academy_uploads/audio/field-agent-verification-yo.mp3',
                    'ig' => '../academy_uploads/audio/field-agent-verification-ig.mp3',
                ],
                'content' => "[CHECKLIST]Daylight panoramic shot showing farm scale and identifiable boundary landmarks\nClose-up photo of bearing palms and young seedlings\nGrower or farm manager present on site (with consent)\nClean document photos with readable text and non-reflective lighting[/CHECKLIST]\n\nPhotos are the visual half of your evidence. A good verification photo set lets a reviewer who has never left their office see the farm clearly: its scale, its condition, its palms, and its people. Learn the standard set and the habits that make photos credible.\n\n[PROTIP]Always shoot between 9:00 AM and 4:00 PM for clean daylight. Photos taken at dusk or indoors result in dark, grainy images that are instantly rejected by the central review desk.[/PROTIP]\n\nQuality rules are simple and strict. Shoot in daylight, hold the phone steady, and frame the subject so it fills the frame. Avoid screenshots, downloaded images, or photos of documents alone; reviewers reject them instantly. Do not reuse old photos, even of the same farm; every photo must be taken during the visit it documents.\n\nContext notes matter as much as images. Write a short, factual caption for each photo: what it shows, where in the farm it was taken, and anything unusual, such as storm damage or a pest outbreak. Facts, not opinions: 'three dead palms in block two' is evidence; 'poor farm' is a judgement that belongs to the reviewer.\n\nKeep the full set together in the visit record before submitting. A missing or mislabelled photo forces a return visit, which costs everyone time. Complete, well-captioned photos are the fastest path to approval.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Farm Visit Workflows',
                'summary' => 'Plan, execute, and close a verification visit end to end.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'video_url' => '../academy_uploads/videos/field-agent-verification-and-gps-evidence-certification-one-minute-brief.html',
                'infographic_url' => 'field-agent-gps-audit',
                'audio_tracks' => [
                    'en' => '../academy_uploads/audio/field-agent-verification-en.mp3',
                    'pcm' => '../academy_uploads/audio/field-agent-verification-pcm.mp3',
                    'ha' => '../academy_uploads/audio/field-agent-verification-ha.mp3',
                    'yo' => '../academy_uploads/audio/field-agent-verification-yo.mp3',
                    'ig' => '../academy_uploads/audio/field-agent-verification-ig.mp3',
                ],
                'content' => "[TAKEAWAY]A structured workflow guarantees complete evidence collection without skipped steps or costly revisit trips.[/TAKEAWAY]\n\nA verification visit is a small project with a beginning, middle, and end. Following a consistent workflow means you never forget a step, and the reviewer receives a complete record the first time.\n\n[STEPS]1. Pre-Trip Dossier Review: Check applicant ID, declared hectarage, and route map before departing.\n2. In-Person Greeting: Meet grower or designated manager and explain the objective of the verification.\n3. Boundary Walk: Walk perimeter alongside grower while collecting GPS coordinates and boundary photos.\n4. Same-Day Submission: Sync the full verification pack before midnight while visit memories are fresh.[/STEPS]\n\n[PROTIP]Never conduct visits unannounced. Confirm grower availability in advance to prevent locked gates and wasted travel.[/PROTIP]\n\nPlan before you travel. Read the profile and the pending verification items: what is being verified, what documents were uploaded, and what the visit objective is. Check the location and plan the route, and confirm the grower expects you. A visit that the grower did not expect often turns into a wasted trip.\n\nExecute with the standard sequence. Confirm identity against the uploaded ID and record the match. Walk the farm with the grower or manager, capture the GPS point and photo set, and verify the declared facts: area, stand count, varieties, and any claimed features. Ask open questions; a grower describing their own farm in detail is itself a signal of authenticity.\n\nHandle problems calmly. If the farm does not match the profile, document exactly what you found: the discrepancy, the evidence, and any explanation offered. Do not accuse, and do not approve to avoid conflict; your record is the evidence the verification team needs to decide. If the grower is absent or refuses the visit, record that too, with the time and the circumstances.\n\nClose the visit the same day. Submit the record with all evidence attached while details are fresh. Flag anything urgent, such as a farm in distress or a safety concern, immediately. A same-day submission protects the quality of your evidence and keeps the verification queue moving for everyone.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Data Quality and Edge Cases',
                'summary' => 'Handle real-world messiness without breaking the record.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'video_url' => '../academy_uploads/videos/field-agent-verification-and-gps-evidence-certification-one-minute-brief.html',
                'infographic_url' => 'field-agent-gps-audit',
                'audio_tracks' => [
                    'en' => '../academy_uploads/audio/field-agent-verification-en.mp3',
                    'pcm' => '../academy_uploads/audio/field-agent-verification-pcm.mp3',
                    'ha' => '../academy_uploads/audio/field-agent-verification-ha.mp3',
                    'yo' => '../academy_uploads/audio/field-agent-verification-yo.mp3',
                    'ig' => '../academy_uploads/audio/field-agent-verification-ig.mp3',
                ],
                'content' => "[TAKEAWAY]Capturing messy field reality accurately is superior to forcing facts into neat boxes that mask truth.[/TAKEAWAY]\n\nField reality is messy: farms without clear entrances, shared family plots, growers without formal IDs, and coordinates that refuse to fix. Data quality is the discipline of capturing that reality accurately rather than forcing it into tidy boxes that hide the truth.\n\n[CHECKLIST]Document alternative name spellings found across certificates and voter cards\nSpecify exact sub-plot locations when verifying shared family estates\nNote physical obstacles (e.g. dense canopy, steep ravines) preventing standard GPS fixes\nRecord verified contact numbers for neighboring plot owners when borders are contested[/CHECKLIST]\n\n[WARNING]Never guess missing coordinates or invent uniform plot dimensions. Unverified guesses corrupt platform GIS layers.[/WARNING]\n\nStart with names and identity. Match loosely but carefully: a grower whose name appears slightly differently across documents is usually the same person, not a fraud. Record the exact spelling on the ID and note the variant you heard. Never invent a spelling to make records match; the discrepancy is the data, and the verification team can resolve it with evidence.\n\nLocation edge cases are common. For shared plots or farms inside larger estates, capture the coordinate at the plot's own identifiable point and describe the relationship in the notes, such as 'plot three of the Okon estate, second gate'. For farms reached only by footpath, use the last reachable point plus a landmark photo, and note the access route.\n\nWhen evidence is impossible to capture, that is itself the finding. Record the obstacle precisely: 'GPS unavailable under dense canopy at the creek plot; photo of marked boundary tree taken instead'. An honest limitation, documented, is far more useful than a confident guess that misplaces the farm.\n\nFinally, keep your own records clean. Submit once, with the correct visit and the correct farm selected; misattached evidence is a data error that can approve the wrong farm. When in doubt, ask the verification team before submitting; a clarifying question costs a minute, and a wrong approval costs a program.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Fraud Detection and Red Flags',
                'summary' => 'Spot the warning signs of fabricated or duplicated farms.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'video_url' => '../academy_uploads/videos/field-agent-verification-and-gps-evidence-certification-one-minute-brief.html',
                'infographic_url' => 'field-agent-gps-audit',
                'audio_tracks' => [
                    'en' => '../academy_uploads/audio/field-agent-verification-en.mp3',
                    'pcm' => '../academy_uploads/audio/field-agent-verification-pcm.mp3',
                    'ha' => '../academy_uploads/audio/field-agent-verification-ha.mp3',
                    'yo' => '../academy_uploads/audio/field-agent-verification-yo.mp3',
                    'ig' => '../academy_uploads/audio/field-agent-verification-ig.mp3',
                ],
                'content' => "[TAKEAWAY]Recognize the key indicators of phantom farms: duplicated GPS, stock photos, lack of cultivated palms, and pressure for rushed approvals.[/TAKEAWAY]\n\nVerification exists because some claims are false. You do not need to be a detective to catch most fraud; you need to know the red flags that fake farms and fabricated documents almost always leave behind.\n\n[WARNING]Fabricating GPS points or reusing old photographs is an automatic termination offense and triggers immediate audit escalation.[/WARNING]\n\n[CHECKLIST]Duplicated coordinates matching previously registered neighboring plots\nEmpty bush or virgin swamp presented as an active bearing palm plantation\nTampered or poorly forged identification documents\nInducement offers, intimidation, or persistent demands for immediate unverified signoff[/CHECKLIST]\n\nThe first red flag is duplication. Two profiles with the same farm photo, the same landmark, or near-identical coordinates are usually one farm split across accounts to double-claim programs. Compare coordinates, photos, and names before concluding a farm is new. If you suspect a duplicate, record the evidence and escalate; do not confront the grower.\n\nThe second red flag is a farm that exists only on paper. No entrance, no activity, no palms matching the declared stand count, or a GPS point that lands on empty bush far from any farm. A real farm shows signs of work: paths, cut grass, tended palms, or people. An empty plot with a perfect profile is a claim waiting to be tested.\n\nThe third red flag is the document. IDs with mismatched photos, blurred text, or inconsistent fonts are common fakes. Farm photos that look like stock images, or images with metadata showing an old date, are another. You do not need to be certain; you need to note the anomaly and let the verification team investigate.\n\nThe fourth red flag is pressure. Anyone who urges you to approve quickly, offers a payment, or claims a special status is testing your integrity. Politely follow the process; the process is the protection. Report pressure attempts immediately, with the person's name and the context. Your independence is the asset the entire program is built on.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Reporting, Escalation and Professional Conduct',
                'summary' => 'Write reports that decisions can be made from.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'video_url' => '../academy_uploads/videos/field-agent-verification-and-gps-evidence-certification-one-minute-brief.html',
                'infographic_url' => 'field-agent-gps-audit',
                'audio_tracks' => [
                    'en' => '../academy_uploads/audio/field-agent-verification-en.mp3',
                    'pcm' => '../academy_uploads/audio/field-agent-verification-pcm.mp3',
                    'ha' => '../academy_uploads/audio/field-agent-verification-ha.mp3',
                    'yo' => '../academy_uploads/audio/field-agent-verification-yo.mp3',
                    'ig' => '../academy_uploads/audio/field-agent-verification-ig.mp3',
                ],
                'content' => "[TAKEAWAY]Clear, factual reporting enables sound verification decisions. State facts objectively, record discrepancies clearly, and escalate irregularities.[/TAKEAWAY]\n\nYour report is the product of the visit. A decision-maker who never travels will approve or reject based on your words and evidence, so reporting is a skill worth mastering: factual, complete, and written the same day.\n\n[STEPS]1. Factual Summary: Detail objective visit findings without personal bias or subjective adjectives.\n2. Discrepancy Log: Record differences between declared profile details and observed field conditions.\n3. Evidence Linking: Ensure all photos and GPS fixes are mapped to corresponding plot sectors.\n4. Formal Escalation: Flag suspected fraudulent claims with detailed incident descriptions.[/STEPS]\n\n[PROTIP]Never accept gifts, hospitality, or cash considerations from farmers during verification visits. Neutrality protects your accreditation.[/PROTIP]\n\nStructure every report the same way. State the objective, what you verified, and the outcome for each item: confirmed, discrepancy, or could not verify. Follow with the evidence list, then any notes and anomalies. End with a clear recommendation only where the process asks for one; otherwise, let the facts speak and the verification team decide.\n\nEscalation is a tool, not an admission of failure. Escalate when you find duplication, suspected fraud, safety concerns, or a situation beyond your authority. A good escalation includes the specific finding, the evidence reference, and why it matters. Vague escalations such as 'something felt off' slow everyone down; precise ones protect the program.\n\nProfessional conduct underpins everything. Be punctual, courteous, and neutral. Explain the visit's purpose to the grower, obtain consent before photographing people, and never accept gifts or payments. Represent the cooperative's values in how you treat every farmer, whether the farm passes or fails.\n\nFinally, protect data. Grower IDs, phone numbers, and coordinates are sensitive; transmit them only through the platform and never through personal chat or social media. The agent who is accurate, prompt, and discreet is the agent the cooperative trusts with its most valuable programs.",
            ],
        ],
        'materials' => [
            ['title' => 'Verification Visit Checklist', 'type' => 'checklist', 'material_url' => '../academy/download-course.php?course_id=7', 'notes' => 'Identity, GPS, photo set, declared facts, and closure steps.'],
            ['title' => 'GPS Evidence Standards Card', 'type' => 'reference', 'material_url' => '../academy/download-course.php?course_id=7', 'notes' => 'Fix quality, landmark tying, and accuracy expectations.'],
            ['title' => 'Photo Evidence Set Guide', 'type' => 'guide', 'material_url' => '../academy/download-course.php?course_id=7', 'notes' => 'Standard photo set, quality rules, and captioning.'],
            ['title' => 'Red Flags Quick Reference', 'type' => 'reference', 'material_url' => '../academy/download-course.php?course_id=7', 'notes' => 'Duplication, paper farms, document anomalies, and pressure.'],
        ],
        'assessment' => [
            'title' => 'Field Agent Verification and GPS Evidence Assessment',
            'instructions' => 'Answer all questions. A score of 70% or higher is required to pass. You have up to 3 attempts.',
            'pass_score' => 70,
            'questions' => [
                ['question' => 'What three things must a field verification confirm?', 'a' => 'Price, quality, and quantity', 'b' => 'Identity, location, and accuracy of declared details', 'c' => 'Soil, water, and sunlight', 'd' => 'Worker names, wages, and hours', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'Why should a GPS fix be taken while standing still in open sky?', 'a' => 'Moving fixes are illegal on the platform', 'b' => 'It gives a stable, accurate fix tied to the farm rather than the road', 'c' => 'It saves phone battery during the visit', 'd' => 'It makes the photo look more professional', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'What is the best response to a farm that does not match its profile?', 'a' => 'Approve it to avoid conflict with the grower', 'b' => 'Confiscate the grower\'s documents on the spot', 'c' => 'Document the discrepancy and evidence, then escalate for decision', 'd' => 'Delete the profile from the field device', 'correct' => 'C'],
                ['question' => 'Which of these is a red flag for a duplicated farm?', 'a' => 'Two profiles with the same landmark photo or near-identical coordinates', 'b' => 'A grower who speaks a different language', 'c' => 'A farm with more than one block', 'd' => 'A profile updated after the harvest season', 'correct' => 'A'],
                ['question' => 'Why must field evidence be transmitted only through the platform?', 'a' => 'Personal chat apps are too slow', 'b' => 'Grower data is sensitive and the platform keeps the audit trail', 'c' => 'The platform compresses photos better', 'd' => 'It is only a suggestion, not a rule', 'correct' => 'B'],
            ],
        ],
    ],

    8 => [
        'overview' => 'Agronomy Advisory Case Management Certification trains agronomists and field officers to run advisory cases from first contact to resolved follow-up. It covers the advisory lifecycle, diagnosis of soil, nutrition, water, pest and disease problems, treatment planning, documentation, safety standards, and scaling advisory work into programs.',
        'duration' => 420,
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'The Agronomy Advisory Lifecycle',
                'summary' => 'Receive, diagnose, recommend, document, and follow up.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'video_url' => '../academy_uploads/videos/agronomy-advisory-case-management-certification-one-minute-brief.html',
                'infographic_url' => 'pest-disease-diagnostics',
                'audio_tracks' => [
                    'en' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-en.mp3',
                    'pcm' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-pcm.mp3',
                    'ha' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-ha.mp3',
                    'yo' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-yo.mp3',
                    'ig' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-ig.mp3',
                ],
                'content' => "[INFOGRAPHIC:pest-disease-diagnostics|Palm Pest & Disease Diagnostics Matrix]\n\n[TAKEAWAY]Follow the full advisory lifecycle: receive, diagnose root causes, recommend measured interventions, document in the system, and follow up.[/TAKEAWAY]\n\nEvery advisory case, from a yellowing palm to a failed nursery batch, follows the same lifecycle: receive, diagnose, recommend, document, and follow up. Managing cases through that lifecycle, rather than answering ad hoc questions, is what turns goodwill into reliable farm outcomes.\n\n[STEPS]1. Intake & Triage: Record affected palm varieties, symptom chronology, and recent weather events.\n2. Root Cause Diagnosis: Differentiate nutrient deficiencies, drainage issues, fungal pathogens, and insect vectors.\n3. Prescriptive Plan: Define specific formulation, measured dosages, PPE requirements, and check-back dates.\n4. Outcome Verification: Visit or inspect evidence on follow-up date to confirm recovery or adjust treatment.[/STEPS]\n\n[PROTIP]Distinguish uniform block symptoms (soil/nutrient issues) from patchy symptoms (localized drainage, pests, or infection centers).[/PROTIP]\n\nReceiving a case well starts with capturing the right information: the contact, the farm or block affected, the crop or palms involved, and the symptoms with their timeline. Ask when the problem started, whether it is spreading, and what has already been tried. A case opened with good information is half-diagnosed before you leave the desk.\n\nDiagnosis is the heart of the case. Distinguish between nutrient problems, water stress, pests, diseases, and mechanical or weather damage, because each demands a different response. Use the farm's history where available: recent applications, weather, and past issues. When you cannot diagnose remotely, schedule a field visit rather than guessing; a wrong diagnosis costs a season, and an honest 'I need to see it' costs a day.\n\nRecommendation follows diagnosis. Prescribe the specific action, product, rate, timing, and safety precautions, and state the expected result and the date to check. Document everything in the case record, because the record is what makes follow-up possible and what proves the advice given.\n\nFollow up closes the loop. Check whether the advice worked, adjust if it did not, and record the outcome. A case is not finished when the message is sent; it is finished when the problem is resolved or escalated. That discipline is what this certification measures.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Diagnosis: Soils, Nutrition and Water',
                'summary' => 'Read the signs of nutrient and water problems in coconut palms.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'video_url' => '../academy_uploads/videos/agronomy-advisory-case-management-certification-one-minute-brief.html',
                'infographic_url' => 'pest-disease-diagnostics',
                'audio_tracks' => [
                    'en' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-en.mp3',
                    'pcm' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-pcm.mp3',
                    'ha' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-ha.mp3',
                    'yo' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-yo.mp3',
                    'ig' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-ig.mp3',
                ],
                'content' => "[TAKEAWAY]Soil, nutrition, and water stress often mimic one another. Differentiating symptoms between older and younger fronds is the fundamental key to accurate diagnosis.[/TAKEAWAY]\n\nSoil, nutrition, and water problems account for a large share of advisory cases, and they often mimic each other. Learning to read the signs, and to separate cause from symptom, is the core diagnostic skill for any agronomist.\n\n[CHECKLIST]Examine older fronds for chlorosis (indicates mobile nutrient deficiencies like nitrogen or magnesium)\nInspect younger spear fronds for curling or necrotic spotting (signals immobile boron or iron deficiencies)\nInspect soil moisture depth: verify root zone drainage vs severe moisture deficit\nCheck frond petiole angle: wilted drooping fronds signal severe drought or root breakage[/CHECKLIST]\n\n[WARNING]Never prescribe chemical fertilizer applications on waterlogged or drought-wilted palms. Applying salts to stressed roots accelerates root necrosis.[/WARNING]\n\nStart with the leaves. Yellowing older fronds often signals nitrogen or magnesium deficiency; yellowing or spotting on younger fronds can point to manganese or boron problems in sensitive soils. Premature nut drop, small nuts, and weak growth are general stress signals with many possible causes. Never treat a symptom without confirming the cause; the same yellowing can come from drought, waterlogging, or a root problem.\n\nSoil context narrows the diagnosis. Sandy, acidic, or heavily leached soils lose nutrients faster and often need targeted correction; heavy clays hold water and can starve roots of oxygen. Where soil tests are available, use them; where they are not, use the farm's history, the variety's known sensitivities, and the pattern across the block. Uniform problems across a whole block suggest soil or nutrition; patchy problems suggest local causes such as drainage or compaction.\n\nWater is the other great mimic. Drought stress shows as wilting and drooping fronds; waterlogging shows as yellowing, poor growth, and sometimes root rot. Check drainage, recent rainfall, and irrigation before concluding a nutrient problem, because fertilising a waterlogged palm makes things worse.\n\nFinally, record your reasoning. Note the signs, the history, and why you ruled alternatives in or out. A documented diagnosis can be reviewed, learned from, and corrected; an undocumented guess repeats itself every season.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Diagnosis: Pests, Diseases and Weeds',
                'summary' => 'Identify the common coconut pests, diseases, and weed pressure.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'video_url' => '../academy_uploads/videos/agronomy-advisory-case-management-certification-one-minute-brief.html',
                'infographic_url' => 'pest-disease-diagnostics',
                'audio_tracks' => [
                    'en' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-en.mp3',
                    'pcm' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-pcm.mp3',
                    'ha' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-ha.mp3',
                    'yo' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-yo.mp3',
                    'ig' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-ig.mp3',
                ],
                'content' => "[TAKEAWAY]Accurate identification of pests and pathogens prevents ineffective chemical applications and preserves beneficial biological controls.[/TAKEAWAY]\n\nPests, diseases, and weeds arrive with characteristic signs, and accurate identification is the whole battle. Misidentification wastes money on the wrong product and loses the season to the real culprit.\n\n[WARNING]Broad-spectrum preventative insecticide sprays decimate beneficial predatory mites and parasitic wasps, triggering secondary scale outbreaks.[/WARNING]\n\n[PROTIP]Rhinoceros beetle damage appears as distinct geometric V-shaped frond cuts. Cultural hygiene and compost pile destruction are far more effective than foliage spraying.[/PROTIP]\n\nLearn the key coconut pests first. The rhinoceros beetle leaves characteristic bore holes and V-shaped cuts in the crown; its grubs live in decaying organic matter, so a clean crown and managed compost are the cultural defence. Leaf-eating caterpillars strip fronds in patches; scale insects appear as immobile bumps on fronds and nuts, often with sooty mould feeding on their honeydew. Each pest has a different control, so identify before you spray.\n\nDiseases follow patterns. Bud rot blackens the emerging spear leaf and often follows wet, injured crowns; leaf rot shows as streaked or rotting fronds, usually in humid, crowded canopies; stem bleeding weeps dark fluid from trunk cracks. Because there are no cures for many palm diseases, prevention, sanitation, and early removal of affected tissue are the primary tools.\n\nWeeds compete for water and nutrients, harbour pests, and complicate harvest. Manage them by slashing, mulching, and targeted weeding around the palm basin, keeping mulch away from the trunk. Herbicides near young palms are risky; if used at all, they must be the labelled product, at the labelled rate, with the right equipment and conditions.\n\nWhen a case does not match a known pattern, escalate for laboratory or specialist diagnosis rather than spraying broadly. Broad sprays kill beneficial insects, waste money, and can leave residues. Precise identification is the cheapest, safest, and most effective control.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Recommendations and Treatment Plans',
                'summary' => 'Prescribe actionable, safe, and affordable treatment plans.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'video_url' => '../academy_uploads/videos/agronomy-advisory-case-management-certification-one-minute-brief.html',
                'infographic_url' => 'pest-disease-diagnostics',
                'audio_tracks' => [
                    'en' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-en.mp3',
                    'pcm' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-pcm.mp3',
                    'ha' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-ha.mp3',
                    'yo' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-yo.mp3',
                    'ig' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-ig.mp3',
                ],
                'content' => "[TAKEAWAY]Treatment plans must be specific, safe, affordable, and actionable. Unclear advice like 'improve nutrition' fails the farmer at their moment of need.[/TAKEAWAY]\n\nA diagnosis is only as good as the recommendation that follows it. A treatment plan must be specific, safe, affordable, and within the farmer's capacity to execute. Vague advice such as 'improve nutrition' or 'spray something' fails the farmer at the moment they need you most.\n\n[STEPS]1. Precise Formulation: Specify exact active ingredient name, registered commercial brand, and dilution ratio.\n2. Safe Application Window: Define application timing to prevent wind drift, rain wash-off, and bee toxicity.\n3. Mandatory PPE Protocols: Require applicators to wear protective face shields, nitrile gloves, and respirators.\n4. Check-Back Date: Schedule a mandatory 14-day field review to assess plant recovery progress.[/STEPS]\n\n[PROTIP]Offer tiered treatment options: a premium full-curative regime alongside an affordable cultural sanitation step for smallholders on restricted budgets.[/PROTIP]\n\nBe specific about the product and the rate. Name the product or formulation, the quantity per palm or hectare, the timing relative to rain or harvest, and the method of application. Where a soil correction is needed, state the material, the rate, and how to apply it around the drip line rather than against the trunk. Specificity turns advice into action.\n\nSafety is non-negotiable in every plan. For any chemical, state the label rate, the required PPE, the pre-harvest interval, and the wind and weather conditions to avoid. Where a cultural or biological control exists, prefer it; where a chemical is justified, the least harmful effective product at the correct dose. Never recommend an unlabelled or unregistered product, and never advise exceeding the label rate.\n\nAffordability decides whether advice is used. Offer options where possible: the ideal correction and a lower-cost interim measure, so a smallholder is never forced between nothing and unaffordable. Sequence the work, starting with the highest-impact, lowest-cost step.\n\nFinally, set the check date. Every plan ends with 'check back in two weeks' and a clear picture of what improvement should look like. A plan without a follow-up date is a hope, not a treatment.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Case Documentation and Follow-Up',
                'summary' => 'Write case records that support follow-up and learning.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'video_url' => '../academy_uploads/videos/agronomy-advisory-case-management-certification-one-minute-brief.html',
                'infographic_url' => 'pest-disease-diagnostics',
                'audio_tracks' => [
                    'en' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-en.mp3',
                    'pcm' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-pcm.mp3',
                    'ha' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-ha.mp3',
                    'yo' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-yo.mp3',
                    'ig' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-ig.mp3',
                ],
                'content' => "[TAKEAWAY]Documentation makes case history searchable, verifiable, and reusable across the agronomy extension network.[/TAKEAWAY]\n\nDocumentation is what makes a case reviewable, a farm's history searchable, and your own expertise reusable. A case record should let any colleague pick it up later and understand exactly what was seen, advised, and decided.\n\n[CHECKLIST]Complete farmer contact details, GPS farm ID, and affected block acreage\nHigh-resolution photographs of healthy vs symptomatic palm foliage and crowns\nDetailed log of previous chemical treatments, pruning, and fertilizer applications\nObjective record of treatment outcome during the 14-day and 30-day follow-up evaluations[/CHECKLIST]\n\n[PROTIP]Never omit unsuccessful treatment outcomes from case logs. Failed interventions provide vital epidemiological clues for regional plant pathologists.[/PROTIP]\n\nStructure the record around the case lifecycle. Capture the contact and farm, the reported problem with its timeline, your observations and diagnosis, the recommendation with product and rate, the safety precautions, and the follow-up date. Attach photos where you have them; a photo of the affected frond is worth a paragraph of description.\n\nWrite facts and separate them from opinion. 'Older fronds yellowing since March, uniform across block two, no recent fertiliser' is a fact pattern. 'The grower is careless' is a judgement that does not belong in the record. The record's job is to support the next decision, not to judge the farmer.\n\nFollow-up is where advisory value is proven. At the follow-up date, check the outcome: did the treatment work, is the problem spreading, or was the diagnosis wrong? Record the result honestly, including cases where your advice failed; those are your most valuable lessons. If the case did not resolve, escalate to a specialist with the full history attached.\n\nWell-kept case records also feed the cooperative's intelligence: recurring problems in a region reveal a trend, such as a pest outbreak or a widespread deficiency, that a program can address. Your documentation turns one farmer's problem into every farmer's prevention.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Advisory Quality and Safety Standards',
                'summary' => 'The professional standards every advisory case must meet.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'video_url' => '../academy_uploads/videos/agronomy-advisory-case-management-certification-one-minute-brief.html',
                'infographic_url' => 'pest-disease-diagnostics',
                'audio_tracks' => [
                    'en' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-en.mp3',
                    'pcm' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-pcm.mp3',
                    'ha' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-ha.mp3',
                    'yo' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-yo.mp3',
                    'ig' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-ig.mp3',
                ],
                'content' => "[TAKEAWAY]Advisory integrity requires ethical independence, safety rigor, and clear awareness of the boundaries of your competence.[/TAKEAWAY]\n\nAdvisory work carries real responsibility: wrong advice can cost a farmer a season, harm consumers, or damage the environment. Quality and safety standards exist so that every case, from the simplest question to the most complex outbreak, is handled to the same professional bar.\n\n[WARNING]Recommending unregistered chemical formulations or off-label rates violates agricultural regulations and subjects advisors to legal liability and accreditation revoking.[/WARNING]\n\n[PROTIP]Verify that farmers actually possess necessary PPE before advising hazardous chemical applications. If PPE is unavailable, prescribe cultural or biological alternatives.[/PROTIP]\n\nThe first standard is competence within your limits. Advise confidently on what you know, and escalate what you do not. A case that needs a laboratory diagnosis, a registered agronomist, or a specialist should be referred, with the record and evidence attached, rather than guessed at. Admitting a limit is a mark of professionalism, not weakness.\n\nThe second standard is safety in every recommendation. This means the labelled product, the labelled rate, the correct PPE, the right weather, and an explicit pre-harvest interval. It also means checking the farmer has, or can obtain, the protective equipment before recommending a chemical. Advice the farmer cannot execute safely is advice that will go wrong.\n\nThe third standard is integrity. Recommendations serve the farmer and the crop, not a product, a supplier, or a commission. Declare any interest, avoid promoting products for reward, and never guarantee results you cannot control. The cooperative's reputation is built on the trustworthiness of its advisors.\n\nThe fourth standard is confidentiality. Farm data, grower identities, and case records are sensitive; share them only with those who need them for the case. The advisor who is competent, safe, honest, and discreet is the one whose advice the cooperative can stand behind.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Scaling Advisory: From Case to Program',
                'summary' => 'Turn recurring cases into programs that help every grower.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'video_url' => '../academy_uploads/videos/agronomy-advisory-case-management-certification-one-minute-brief.html',
                'infographic_url' => 'pest-disease-diagnostics',
                'audio_tracks' => [
                    'en' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-en.mp3',
                    'pcm' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-pcm.mp3',
                    'ha' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-ha.mp3',
                    'yo' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-yo.mp3',
                    'ig' => '../academy_uploads/audio/agronomy-advisory-ig.mp3',
                ],
                'content' => "[TAKEAWAY]A single case fixes one farm; an aggregated program fixes an entire growing region. Transform recurring advisory cases into cooperative-wide training modules.[/TAKEAWAY]\n\nA single case fixes one farm; a program fixes a region. The final skill of the agronomist is recognising when individual cases reveal a pattern, and turning that pattern into a program the cooperative can run.\n\n[STEPS]1. Pattern Detection: Aggregate case records across LGAs to spot recurring nutrient shortages or pest outbreaks.\n2. Curriculum Design: Build clear, evidence-based training modules, reference cards, and practical field demos.\n3. Extension Training: Train local cooperative field officers and community leaders to disseminate preventative protocols.\n4. Regional Impact Measurement: Measure disease incidence reduction and overall yield recovery across target zones.[/STEPS]\n\n[PROTIP]Translate complex technical agronomy insights into plain local language bulletins and visual infographics for community bulletin boards.[/PROTIP]\n\nWatch for recurrence. The same deficiency appearing across many farms in one LGA, the same pest arriving each season, the same nursery mistake repeated by new growers: these are signals that a program, a training course, or a bulletin will help more people than individual advice ever could. Record cases consistently so the pattern is visible in the data.\n\nDesign programs around the evidence. A nutrition program needs the deficient nutrient, the affected areas, the correction material, and the timing. A pest program needs the pest's lifecycle, the monitoring method, and the threshold that justifies action. Anchor the design in your documented cases, and state the expected outcome and how it will be measured.\n\nCommunicate at the right level. Growers need clear, actionable guidance; coordinators need the evidence and the plan; buyers and regulators need the outcomes. Translate your case knowledge into each audience's language, and train the trainers who will carry the message to the fields.\n\nFinally, evaluate the program. Compare the target metrics before and after: yield, incidence, or cost. Feed the results back into your case knowledge, and improve the program each cycle. The agronomist who scales well leaves the whole cooperative stronger, not just the farms they happened to visit.",
            ],
        ],
        'materials' => [
            ['title' => 'Advisory Case Template', 'type' => 'template', 'material_url' => '../academy/download-course.php?course_id=8', 'notes' => 'Contact, farm, symptoms, timeline, diagnosis, plan, follow-up date.'],
            ['title' => 'Nutrient and Water Sign Chart', 'type' => 'reference', 'material_url' => '../academy/download-course.php?course_id=8', 'notes' => 'Leaf signs, soil context, and water-stress indicators.'],
            ['title' => 'Pest and Disease Identification Card', 'type' => 'reference', 'material_url' => '../academy/download-course.php?course_id=8', 'notes' => 'Key coconut pests and diseases with first actions.'],
            ['title' => 'Treatment Plan Builder', 'type' => 'template', 'material_url' => '../academy/download-course.php?course_id=8', 'notes' => 'Product, rate, timing, PPE, pre-harvest interval, check date.'],
        ],
        'assessment' => [
            'title' => 'Agronomy Advisory Case Management Assessment',
            'instructions' => 'Answer all questions. A score of 70% or higher is required to pass. You have up to 3 attempts.',
            'pass_score' => 70,
            'questions' => [
                ['question' => 'What is the correct order of the advisory case lifecycle?', 'a' => 'Recommend, receive, diagnose, document, follow up', 'b' => 'Receive, diagnose, recommend, document, follow up', 'c' => 'Diagnose, follow up, receive, recommend, document', 'd' => 'Document, receive, recommend, diagnose, follow up', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'Yellowing older fronds most often signal which problem?', 'a' => 'A nitrogen or magnesium deficiency', 'b' => 'A rhinoceros beetle infestation', 'c' => 'Bud rot disease', 'd' => 'An aphid outbreak', 'correct' => 'A'],
                ['question' => 'Why should an advisor escalate instead of guessing at an unknown pattern?', 'a' => 'Escalation is always faster than diagnosis', 'b' => 'A wrong diagnosis can cost a season; a referral costs a day', 'c' => 'Advisors are not allowed to diagnose anything', 'd' => 'Guessing is only wrong for paid courses', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'What must every chemical recommendation include?', 'a' => 'The cheapest product and a promise of results', 'b' => 'Label rate, PPE, timing, and pre-harvest interval', 'c' => 'The supplier\'s phone number and a discount code', 'd' => 'A double dose to be safe', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'How do individual cases become a program?', 'a' => 'By ignoring one-off cases and only tracking complaints', 'b' => 'By spotting recurring patterns in documented cases and designing around the evidence', 'c' => 'By waiting for government funding first', 'd' => 'By treating every case as unique and unrelated', 'correct' => 'B'],
            ],
        ],
    ],

    9 => [
        'overview' => 'Input Provider Compliance and Product Listing Certification trains input providers to operate legally and safely on the marketplace. It covers the regulatory landscape, product registration and labelling, storage and stock integrity, listing standards, pricing and claims discipline, responsible sale, and audit readiness.',
        'duration' => 420,
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Regulatory Landscape for Agricultural Inputs',
                'summary' => 'Know the rules that govern input products and their sale.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Agricultural inputs are regulated because they carry real risk: wrong product, wrong rate, or counterfeit product can destroy a crop, poison a worker, or contaminate food. As an input provider, you operate inside that regulatory landscape, and this lesson maps what it means for your business.\n\nStart with the product side. Pesticides, fertilisers, and other crop inputs are subject to registration and labelling rules in the markets where they are sold. A registered product has an approved label stating its purpose, formulation, rate, safety precautions, and pre-harvest interval. Selling unregistered or mislabelled products is illegal and is the fastest way to lose accreditation and customers alike.\n\nNext is the seller side. Providers are expected to verify the products they stock, keep source documentation such as supplier invoices, and be able to show that their supply chain is legitimate. Counterfeit and unregistered inputs are a persistent problem in agricultural markets; your documentation is the proof that your stock is genuine.\n\nThen there is the advisory boundary. Input providers may describe products accurately and explain label directions, but they are not agronomists unless qualified. Know the difference between explaining a label and prescribing a treatment; the latter is professional advice that carries its own responsibilities. When in doubt, direct the grower to a certified advisor.\n\nFinally, track the landscape. Registration rules, banned substances, and label requirements change. Subscribe to the relevant authority's notices, and review your stock whenever a restriction is announced. The provider who stays current is the provider whose accreditation survives audits.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Product Registration and Labelling Compliance',
                'summary' => 'Verify registration, read labels, and stock only compliant products.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Registration and labelling are the two gates a product must pass before it reaches your shelf. Checking them is not bureaucracy; it is the barrier that keeps dangerous and ineffective products away from growers.\n\nVerify registration before you stock. For each product, confirm it is registered for the stated purpose and crop in your market, and that the registration is current. Keep a copy of the registration or the supplier's confirmation with your product file. A product you cannot verify is a product you cannot sell, however attractive the margin.\n\nRead the label as your first line of defence. The label tells you the formulation, the target pests or crops, the rate, the application method, the PPE, the pre-harvest interval, and the storage and disposal rules. Check that the label is intact, legible, and in a language your customers understand; products with missing, damaged, or illegible labels must not be sold.\n\nWatch for the classic warning signs: unlabelled or repackaged products, labels that do not match the contents, products with foreign or unfamiliar names and no local registration, and 'me-too' products with exaggerated claims. Any of these is reason to reject the stock and report the supplier.\n\nMaintain a product file per stock-keeping unit: registration evidence, label copy, supplier invoice, and expiry date. The product file is what an inspector asks for first, and it is also your own defence if a product is ever questioned. Compliance done at intake prevents problems at sale.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Storage, Handling and Stock Integrity',
                'summary' => 'Store inputs safely so products stay effective and safe.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "How you store inputs is as important as what you sell. Poor storage degrades products, creates hazards, and can turn a compliant product into a dangerous one. Storage discipline protects your customers, your staff, and your business.\n\nSeparate and secure. Keep inputs in a dedicated, lockable store, away from food, feed, water sources, and public access. Segregate chemicals from fertilisers, and keep different chemical classes apart where the label requires it. The store should be ventilated, dry, and shaded, because heat and moisture degrade many products and can damage containers.\n\nRespect the label's storage instructions. Some products require specific temperature ranges or must be kept away from certain materials. Follow them exactly, and check that containers are intact, sealed, and correctly labelled. Damaged, leaking, or unlabelled containers are quarantined immediately, not sold or decanted into food containers.\n\nManage stock rotation and expiry. Sell oldest stock first, monitor expiry dates, and never sell expired products; an expired product may have lost potency or degraded into something unsafe. Record your stock with batch and expiry so a recall can be answered quickly and precisely, rather than by guesswork.\n\nPrepare for incidents. Keep spill kits and fire equipment appropriate to your stock, train staff on the emergency procedure, and post the emergency contacts. A well-run store is quiet, orderly, and ready for the unlikely day when something goes wrong.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Listing Standards and Marketplace Compliance',
                'summary' => 'List products so buyers can find, filter, and trust them.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Your marketplace listings are your shop window and your compliance statement together. A listing must be findable, precise, and truthful, because buyers filter by category and formulation, and reviewers verify listings against your registration documents.\n\nBuild listings with precision. The title names the product, its formulation or grade, and the unit; the category matches the product type so agronomists and growers can filter for it. Vague listings such as 'good fertilizer' or 'cheap spray' are returned for correction, and they attract the wrong inquiries besides.\n\nDescribe the product accurately. State what it is for, the formulation, the package size, and any registration or certification that applies. Never claim a product does something its label does not support; exaggerated efficacy claims are both a marketplace violation and a regulatory breach.\n\nPrice and stock honestly. List your real current price and your real availability, and update promptly when either changes. Bait pricing and phantom stock damage buyer trust and count against your reliability score, which affects your visibility in search.\n\nKeep evidence ready. Reviewers sample-check listings against registration and invoices, so your product files must be current and retrievable. A listing set that is complete, accurately priced, honestly stocked, and backed by documents is the fastest path through accreditation and the slowest to be suspended.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Pricing, Claims and Advertising Discipline',
                'summary' => 'Price honestly and advertise within the rules.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Pricing and advertising are where trust is won or lost. The rules are simple: real prices, truthful claims, and no manipulation. Providers who break them lose accreditation, customers, and sometimes their legal standing.\n\nPrice at your real market. Check competing listings for the same product and grade, and set a price you can honour. If input costs move, update the listing rather than hiding the change behind a conversation; a buyer who sees a different price at pickup feels cheated, and the complaint follows you.\n\nAvoid bait pricing entirely. Advertising a low price to attract inquiries and then negotiating upward is a marketplace violation that can suspend the listing. The same applies to phantom discounts and 'limited offer' pressure that does not reflect reality. Honest pricing builds repeat business, which is worth more than a one-time inquiry.\n\nKeep claims truthful and verifiable. Claims about yield, speed of action, or safety must be supported by the product's registration and label. Never claim a product is 'the strongest', 'guaranteed', or 'safe for everything'; absolute claims are red flags to reviewers and regulators. Describe what the product is and does, per its label, and let its record speak.\n\nFinally, keep your advertising consistent with your coverage and stock. Do not advertise nationwide delivery when you serve two states, and do not advertise a product you cannot supply. Consistency between listing, stock, and delivery is the discipline that keeps your store credible.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Customer Advice and Responsible Sale',
                'summary' => 'Guide buyers accurately and refuse unsafe requests.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "The moment of sale is where your responsibility is most real. A provider who advises accurately protects the crop, the grower, and the consumer; a provider who guesses can cause harm that follows the product back to the shelf.\n\nExplain, do not prescribe. You may accurately describe the product, its formulation, and its label directions, and you may show the grower where the rate and precautions are printed. You may not diagnose the grower's problem and prescribe a treatment unless you hold the professional qualification to do so. The boundary is simple: the label is yours to explain; the diagnosis belongs to a certified advisor.\n\nAsk before you recommend anything beyond the label. If a grower describes a problem, the responsible answer is to ask what the product's label says for that use, and to suggest they confirm with an agronomist if the situation is unclear. Selling the wrong product confidently is worse than selling nothing.\n\nRefuse unsafe requests politely but firmly. Requests for unregistered products, higher-than-label rates, or products for unlisted crops should be declined, with the reason explained. Offer the compliant alternative where one exists. A refusal documented with the reason protects you and educates the grower.\n\nKeep a sale record: product, quantity, buyer, and date. For regulated products, the record is your traceability. It lets you answer a recall, support an investigation, and prove that your sale was within the rules. Responsible sale is the daily habit that makes accreditation meaningful.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Audits, Inspections and Continuous Compliance',
                'summary' => 'Stay audit-ready and improve compliance over time.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Compliance is a state, not an event. Audits and inspections can arrive with short notice, and the providers who pass them are the ones who treat readiness as a daily routine rather than a pre-visit scramble.\n\nBuild the compliance file and keep it current: business registration, tax identifier, product files with registration evidence and labels, supplier invoices, stock records with batch and expiry, and any incident reports. An inspector's first request is usually for exactly this file, and producing it in minutes sets the tone for the visit.\n\nRun your own internal audit on a schedule. Monthly, walk the store against the listing standards: labels intact, stock rotated, expired products removed, store secure and ventilated, records complete. Note the gaps, fix them, and record the fix. The self-audit is cheaper than the inspection finding and builds the muscle of readiness.\n\nHandle findings professionally. If an inspector identifies a gap, correct it promptly and document the correction. Denial and deflection turn a small finding into a serious one; a documented, timely fix demonstrates a functioning compliance culture.\n\nFinally, close the loop with your team. Train staff on the rules that affect their role, from storage to sale records, and make compliance part of the daily routine rather than the manager's private worry. A provider whose whole team understands the rules is the provider that stays accredited, trusted, and in business.",
            ],
        ],
        'materials' => [
            ['title' => 'Product File Template', 'type' => 'template', 'material_url' => '../academy/download-course.php?course_id=9', 'notes' => 'Registration evidence, label copy, invoice, expiry per SKU.'],
            ['title' => 'Input Store Checklist', 'type' => 'checklist', 'material_url' => '../academy/download-course.php?course_id=9', 'notes' => 'Separation, security, ventilation, rotation, and incident readiness.'],
            ['title' => 'Listing Standards Quick Card', 'type' => 'reference', 'material_url' => '../academy/download-course.php?course_id=9', 'notes' => 'Title, category, formulation, unit, price, and stock discipline.'],
            ['title' => 'Responsible Sale Script', 'type' => 'guide', 'material_url' => '../academy/download-course.php?course_id=9', 'notes' => 'Explain vs prescribe, and how to refuse unsafe requests.'],
        ],
        'assessment' => [
            'title' => 'Input Provider Compliance and Product Listing Assessment',
            'instructions' => 'Answer all questions. A score of 70% or higher is required to pass. You have up to 3 attempts.',
            'pass_score' => 70,
            'questions' => [
                ['question' => 'What must an input provider verify before stocking a product?', 'a' => 'That the product is registered for its stated purpose and the label is intact and legible', 'b' => 'That the product is the cheapest on the market', 'c' => 'That the supplier is a friend of the provider', 'd' => 'That the product has a colourful label', 'correct' => 'A'],
                ['question' => 'What is the provider\'s boundary when a grower describes a pest problem?', 'a' => 'Diagnose the pest and prescribe the strongest product', 'b' => 'Explain the product and its label, and refer diagnosis to a certified advisor', 'c' => 'Refuse to speak to the grower at all', 'd' => 'Recommend double the label rate to be safe', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'Why is stock rotation with batch and expiry records important?', 'a' => 'It makes the store look tidy for photos', 'b' => 'It prevents expired products being sold and enables fast recall', 'c' => 'It is required only for fertilisers', 'd' => 'It lets the provider raise prices', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'What is bait pricing?', 'a' => 'Pricing at the real market rate', 'b' => 'Advertising a low price to attract inquiries and then negotiating upward', 'c' => 'Offering a discount to loyal customers', 'd' => 'Matching a competitor\'s sale price', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'What is the best response to an audit finding?', 'a' => 'Deny the finding and argue with the inspector', 'b' => 'Correct it promptly and document the correction', 'c' => 'Close the store until the inspector leaves', 'd' => 'Delete the records that show the gap', 'correct' => 'B'],
            ],
        ],
    ],

    10 => [
        'overview' => 'Service Provider Operations and Quality Assurance trains service providers to deliver consistent, professional farm services. It covers operations design, quality standards, field teams, equipment and logistics, customer communication, quality measurement, and continuous improvement that scales with demand.',
        'duration' => 420,
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Service Operations Design',
                'summary' => 'Design a service that can be delivered consistently at scale.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "A service business lives or dies on consistency: the same quality, on time, whether the job is your first or your fiftieth. Operations design is the discipline of making that consistency repeatable, so it does not depend on one person's memory or heroics.\n\nStart by defining the service precisely. What exactly do you deliver, to whom, where, and to what standard? A service described as 'pruning' means nothing until you specify the palms covered, the method, the safety standards, and the expected outcome. Precise definition is what lets you price, staff, and deliver reliably.\n\nMap the delivery process end to end: booking, scheduling, travel, setup, execution, quality check, and handover. For each step, note who does it, what they need, and how long it takes. The map reveals your bottlenecks before your customers do, and it becomes the basis for training every new team member.\n\nBuild the service around standards, not around individuals. Write the standard method for each core job, including safety steps and the quality bar. When the method is written, anyone you hire can be trained to it, and customers experience the same service regardless of who arrives at their farm.\n\nFinally, design for the customer's reality. Farm services happen around weather, harvest windows, and the customer's availability. Build scheduling flexibility and clear communication into the process, because a service that ignores the farm calendar will always disappoint.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Quality Standards and Service Delivery',
                'summary' => 'Set the bar for quality and deliver to it every time.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Quality is what customers remember and what they recommend. In service work, quality is not a slogan; it is a set of observable standards that you deliver to on every job, and that the customer can see and judge.\n\nDefine quality in observable terms. For a pruning service: clean cuts at the right height, no damage to the trunk, the area left tidy. For a spraying service: full coverage at the labelled rate, no drift, the field logged. The standard must be specific enough that two different teams would produce the same result.\n\nDeliver to the standard through a checklist. The pre-job checklist covers equipment, materials, PPE, and the site walkthrough; the in-job checklist covers the method steps; the post-job checklist covers the quality inspection and the customer sign-off. Checklists are not a sign of low skill; they are how professionals make sure nothing is skipped.\n\nHandle variation honestly. Weather, soil, and farm conditions change, and the standard may need adjusting; when it does, agree the change with the customer and note it in the job record. What you never do is quietly lower the standard and hope the customer does not notice.\n\nClose every job with a short quality review: what was done, what varied, and the customer's confirmation. This record is your evidence of delivery, your improvement data, and the basis of the customer's trust in your reliability score.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Staffing, Training and Field Teams',
                'summary' => 'Hire, train, and keep a team that delivers your standard.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Your service is delivered by people, so your team is your quality. Staffing well means hiring for reliability, training to the standard, and keeping good people, because every employee who leaves takes customers' trust with them.\n\nHire for the fundamentals: punctuality, honesty, physical fitness for field work, and willingness to follow safety rules. Skills can be taught; reliability and integrity cannot. Check references where you can, and hire with the standard in mind: will this person deliver it consistently, unsupervised?\n\nTrain to the written method. New staff start with safety, then the standard method for each core job, then the evidence and communication expectations. Train with a checklist and a supervised first job; never send a new worker out alone on a task they have only read about. Training is an investment that pays back in fewer reworks and fewer incidents.\n\nKeep the team with fair pay, prompt payment, and clear expectations. Field workers who are paid fairly and on time stay longer and care more about the quality of their work. Listen to their reports from the field; they see problems before you do, and a team that speaks up is a team that prevents disasters.\n\nFinally, document your training. A training record per worker proves competence during audits and insurance checks, and it tells you who is qualified for which job. The provider with a trained, documented, stable team can scale; the provider with ad hoc labour cannot.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Equipment, Logistics and Scheduling',
                'summary' => 'The right tools, at the right place, at the right time.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Service delivery runs on equipment and logistics: having the right tools, in working order, at the right farm, at the right time. Failures here are the most common cause of missed jobs and unhappy customers.\n\nMaintain equipment before it fails. Keep a maintenance log for each tool and machine: service dates, repairs, and the next check. Inspect equipment before each job; a faulty sprayer or a dull blade discovered at the farm is a lost day and a damaged reputation. Spare parts for the critical items are cheap insurance against a broken job.\n\nPlan logistics around geography. Group jobs by area to cut travel time and cost, and plan routes with realistic travel times between farms. A schedule that ignores travel is a schedule that is always late. Keep a daily job list with locations, times, and the materials each job needs, and stage the vehicle the evening before.\n\nSchedule around the farm calendar. Respect harvest windows, weather, and the customer's availability, and confirm each job the day before. Confirmations cut no-shows, and no-shows are the quiet killer of service profitability: the travel cost is paid either way.\n\nTrack what actually happened versus the plan: jobs completed, delays, and their causes. The pattern in your records tells you whether the problem is equipment, routing, or scheduling, and it points directly at the fix.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Customer Communication and Expectations',
                'summary' => 'Set expectations clearly and keep them at every step.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Most service complaints are not about the work; they are about surprises. Clear communication before, during, and after the job is the cheapest quality tool you have, and it is what turns a one-time customer into a repeat one.\n\nBefore the job, confirm the scope, the price, the date, and what the customer should prepare. Put it in writing, even a simple message, so there is no argument later about what was agreed. A written record protects both sides and makes your quote defensible.\n\nDuring the job, communicate changes immediately. If weather, access, or a discovered problem changes the plan, tell the customer before they find out from someone else. A delay explained early is a delay managed; a delay discovered by the customer is a dispute.\n\nAt handover, walk the customer through the result against the agreed standard, and ask for their confirmation. Fix small things on the spot where you can; a customer who watches you correct a minor issue right in front of them trusts you more than one who promises to return.\n\nAfter the job, follow up within a day or two to confirm satisfaction, and ask for feedback. Feedback, positive or negative, is data: it tells you what to repeat and what to fix. The provider who communicates at every step rarely faces a surprise complaint, and rarely loses a good customer.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Measuring Quality: KPIs and Reviews',
                'summary' => 'Measure the service with numbers that matter.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "You cannot improve what you do not measure. A small set of key performance indicators tells you whether your service is getting better, where it is failing, and what to fix next. Choose a few measures that reflect what the customer actually cares about.\n\nStart with the customer-facing measures: on-time arrival rate, first-time completion rate, rework rate, and customer satisfaction or repeat rate. These four numbers describe the experience customers have, and they are the measures that drive your marketplace rating and word of mouth.\n\nAdd the operational measures that explain the customer numbers: jobs per day, travel time per job, equipment downtime, and incidents or near-misses. If on-time arrival is falling, the operational data shows whether the cause is routing, equipment, or scheduling. Customer symptoms, operational causes: that is the relationship your dashboard should reveal.\n\nReview the numbers on a fixed rhythm: weekly for operations, monthly for the full set. A weekly fifteen-minute review of last week's jobs, delays, and incidents catches problems while they are small. A monthly review of trends tells you whether a fix actually worked.\n\nFinally, share the numbers with your team. Workers who see the measures understand what good looks like and own the result. A team that measures together improves together, and improvement compounds into the reputation that fills your booking calendar.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Continuous Improvement and Scaling',
                'summary' => 'Get better every month and grow without breaking quality.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Continuous improvement is the habit of making the service a little better every month, and scaling is the art of growing without letting quality slip. The two go together: a service that improves can scale; a service that stands still cannot.\n\nImprove from evidence. Use your job records, customer feedback, and KPI trends to pick one improvement per month: a bottleneck, a recurring complaint, a step that always runs late. Change the method, train the team, and check the numbers next month. Small, measured changes beat annual overhauls every time.\n\nStandardise before you scale. Every new area, vehicle, or team member should repeat the same written method, checklist, and communication standard. Scaling by adding people without standardising is how quality collapses; the written standard is what lets you grow while customers feel nothing change.\n\nGrow capacity deliberately. Add a team or a vehicle only when the current one is reliably full, and only after the standard is documented and trainable. Over-expansion is the classic service failure: the jobs grow faster than the trained people, and quality falls while costs rise.\n\nFinally, protect the culture. Your reputation is your team's habits, multiplied by every job. As you grow, keep hiring for reliability, keep training to the standard, and keep listening to the field workers who see the truth first. The provider that improves continuously earns the right to scale.",
